::ENG3_U01_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses academic vocabulary most precisely? {
~A. The author does a good thing with examples.#Incorrect. This wording is vague.
=B. The author uses specific examples to support a claim about public responsibility.#Correct. This uses precise academic vocabulary.
~C. The author has stuff that sounds important.#Incorrect. Stuff is too vague for analysis.
~D. The author writes words that are nice.#Incorrect. Nice does not explain the author&apos;s choice.
}

::ENG3_U01_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] A student wants to discuss how repeated words affect an audience. Which vocabulary word is most useful? {
~A. setting#Incorrect. Setting names time and place, not repeated words.
~B. plot#Incorrect. Plot names events in a narrative.
=C. repetition#Correct. Repetition names the repeated language.
~D. summary#Incorrect. Summary is a shortened restatement, not a technique.
}

::ENG3_U01_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which revision best replaces vague wording? Weak sentence: The speech makes people feel like they should act now. {
~A. The speech is good and has a strong feeling.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
~B. The speech has things that make people do stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~C. The speech says many words about action.#Incorrect. This does not name the effect precisely.
=D. The speech creates urgency by calling the audience to immediate action.#Correct. Urgency and immediate action precisely name the effect.
}

::ENG3_U01_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which word best names a writer&apos;s viewpoint or position? {
=A. perspective#Correct. Perspective means viewpoint or position.
~B. capitalization#Incorrect. Capitalization is a convention.
~C. transition#Incorrect. A transition connects ideas.
~D. punctuation#Incorrect. Punctuation marks sentence structure.
}

::ENG3_U01_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence correctly uses the word evaluate? {
~A. Evaluate means to copy the passage word for word.#Incorrect. Evaluate does not mean copy.
~B. The reader evaluates the text by ignoring evidence.#Incorrect. Evaluation requires evidence.
=C. The reader evaluates the argument by judging the strength of its evidence.#Correct. Evaluate means judge using evidence.
~D. The reader evaluates by writing an unrelated opinion.#Incorrect. Evaluation must connect to evidence.
}

